# mt\_tool Architecture & Reference

Oct 15, 2026 · @phoonsz

mt\_tool converts GTA V ped-metadata files between the console binary layout (`.cmt`), the PC binary layout (`.ymt`), and a portable XML form, preserving the original bytes wherever the edit allows it.

## Overview

A file is a 16-byte outer header followed by a raw-deflate stream. The inflated body holds a header, schema tables (struct/enum/block descriptors), and one or more data blocks containing the actual tree. Pointers inside blocks pack a block index and an offset into a single integer; table pointers are a flat `SYSTEM_BASE + body offset`.

| Format | Extension | Layout |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Console | `.cmt` | big-endian, 4-byte pointers |
| PC | `.ymt` | little-endian, 8-byte pointers |
| Portable | `.xml` | layout-neutral; the source layout is recorded in the root tag |

Three conversions are supported: binary → XML (for inspection and editing), XML → binary (writing edits back), and binary/XML → the other platform's binary layout.

## Architecture

&#91;embedded content: conversion pipeline · one tree, three output paths\]

Edited XML with a body index and no layout change takes `chunks_to_body`, the only path that reproduces an unedited file byte-for-byte; everything else rebuilds every struct's layout from scratch.

## Conversion paths

Which internal function builds the output body depends on the input/output combination:

| Input | Output | Function | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `.cmt`/`.ymt` | `.xml` | `tree_to_xml` | Schema, tree and a byte-level body index (`<_Body>`) are written out |
| `.xml` (same layout, has `<_Body>`) | `.cmt`/`.ymt` | `chunks_to_body` | The original body is reconstructed from the index, then the schema tables and tree are patched in |
| `.xml` (no `<_Body>`, or cross-layout) | `.cmt`/`.ymt` | `rebuild_body` | Every struct is laid out from scratch and the whole tree is written into one new block |
| `.cmt` ↔ `.ymt` (different layout) | `.cmt`/`.ymt` | `rebuild_body` | Same as above; same-layout pairs instead recompress the original body unchanged |

`chunks_to_body` is the only path that reproduces the original file byte-for-byte when nothing was edited, since `rebuild_body` always recomputes struct layouts.

## Binary format reference

**Header (0x50 bytes)**

| Offset | Field |
| --- | --- |
| 0x1C | Root block index (1-based) |
| 0x20 | Struct table pointer |
| 0x28 | Enum table pointer |
| 0x30 | Block table pointer |
| 0x48 | Struct count (u16) |
| 0x4A | Enum count (u16) |
| 0x4C | Block count (u16) |

**Struct table record (32 bytes)**: name hash at 0x00, entries pointer at 0x10, size at 0x18, entry count (u16) at 0x1E. Each entry is 16 bytes: name hash, offset (u32), type (u8), key (u32).

**Enum table record (24 bytes)**: name hash at 0x00, values pointer at 0x08, value count at 0x10 (u16 on console, u32 on PC). Each value is 8 bytes: name hash, signed value.

**Block table record (16 bytes)**: name hash, length (u32), data pointer.

**Block pointers**: the low 12 bits hold `block index + 1` (0 means null); the next 20 bits hold the byte offset inside that block.

## Command-line usage

```
mt_tool <input> <output>
```

The extension of each path selects its format: `.cmt` is console, `.ymt` is PC, `.xml` is either (the layout is read from or written to the root tag). The two formats can't both be `.xml`. Examples:

```
mt_tool player_0.ymt player_0.xml   # PC binary to XML
mt_tool player_0.xml player_0.cmt   # edited XML to console binary
mt_tool player_0.ymt player_0.cmt   # PC to console, rebuilding the layout
```
